A||||15||Ken Wittlief||brake problem - flexing vacuum assist booster||||1||
Z||000000||Ken Wittlief||06-18-2001||11:21 AM||||I was ready to tear into my '85SE this weekend and take another wack at the brakes, and I couldnt convince myself that any of the things I was planning on doing would resolve my soft brakes.<P>  so I had my son get in the car, and I watched the vacuum booster, and told him to step on the brake pedal. It flexed just a little bit, the master cylinder moving maybe 1/16th of an inch.<P>  I noticed his left foot was still out of the car, so I told him, push it with both feet, right to the floor.<P>   and now I think I know what is wrong with my brakes: the master cylinder side of the booster moved at least a 1/4 of an inch - maybe more - Ill have to measure it to be precise.<P>  this seems to make sense with how my brakes feel. the pedal is hard with the engine off, I am sure there is no air in the lines, they dont pump up, and I have replaced the master cylinder.<P>  but when I start the engine I can push the pedal to the floor, and I cant lock up my tires at any speed.<P>   has anyone ever had this happen before? I guess it doesnt matter how hard the vacuum assist is pushing against the piston in the master cylinder, if the WHOLE master cylinder moves when it does.<P>   I dont know if something is broken inside it, or if its gotten weak over the years, or maybe when PO hit a deer he nailed the brakes so hard the vacuum booster was flexed/damaged/broken inside.<P>  for a sanity check I did the same test on my saturn 2000 SL. The mastercylinder moves very slightly, about 1/16th of an inch or so, even with the pedal pressed as hard as possible.<P>   can anyone else do this with their car too? I dont see how the vacuum assist can apply any more pressure to the master cylinder piston if the whole thing moves. So even though this was one of my 'maybe this is the problem with my brakes' ideas, Im really surprized to see it acutally doing this.||||reg||1
Z||000001||SteveJ||06-18-2001||11:43 AM||||Ken, I bet you have air in your lines. Here's why. I went out to Illinois to replace the brake lines on my son's 84. By the time we got done with it we had to do all four brake lines front to back. ( we did this in the parking lot of his apt by the way ) Front end bled easily. So did the rear. We tried it and the petal went to just above the floor and the MC rocked back and fourth like you saw on yours only maybe more movement.<BR>Well we tightened some of the fittings and bled the rears again and the petal came up to normal. Funny thing about that, the MC stopped jumping around too.<P>. . .and on its best day that car has never locked up any brake even with new pads and rotors.||||reg||1
Z||000002||SteveJ||06-18-2001||11:52 AM||||Ya know I too have had disquietening feelings about those brakes and that power assist in particular. I've gone so far as to pull the vacuum line, plug it and then drive the car. Well it proved to me the thing was working. I don't recommend it. The car is just plain dangerous without the power assist.<BR>So the booster does work and it doesn't display any classic MC problems so I guess its just crappy poor brakes due to bad design.<BR>But I'm with you on that MC jumping around thing 'cause I've seen it and cured it with a good bleed, or let us say its down to that 1/16 movement you saw on the Saturn.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ken Wittlief||06-18-2001||11:52 AM||||well thats confusing. With the engine off I have a hard pedal, its hard about half way down - there is no sense of spongyness to it at all, and if I pump it it doesnt get any higher.<P>  But if I press it with both feet - Im talking really hard here, it will go to the floor. (and thats when the master cylinder assembly is moving cause the booster front side is flexing like bellows).<P>  If its air in the lines then maybe the rear half is compressing the air and causing the master cylinder to bottom out - so if you push it harder it has nowhere to go but to  flex the booster assembly?<P>   Somehow this doenst seem right - I would not expect the pedal to have enough travel to bottom the master cylinder out, and then push it twice as far again.<P>  but right now I would believe anything.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ken Wittlief||06-18-2001||12:00 PM||||but on the other hand, if you have had a similar experience, then maybe its not easy to understand why, but bleeding the lines might fix it. I was thinking of taking the rear calipers off their bolts and rotating them so any trapped air can rise to the bleeders, and then bleeding the rear again. <P>  I should point out I have bled a whole quart of brake fluid all the way though all four brake lines, using a vacuum bleeder pump, till I got clean fluid at all four wheels. And that was the second time I bled the brakes.<P> Maybe Ill give it another shot - third times the charm, right?||||reg||1
Z||000005||SteveJ||06-18-2001||12:06 PM||||Ya know I can't reason it out. I have been disapointed with the brakes for a long time and they don't behave like any others I have ever delt with. I can only say I've seen that MC moving thing during the course of line replacement and that it was bleeding that cured it. And for us it was the rear line that was the problem.<BR>It's a single 1/4" line from the proporting valve to a "Y" block above the passenger rear tire where it's normal size line to each wheel. Bleeding should begin at the driver's rear wheel then the passenger rear.<BR>Maybe it has to do with the change in line size or maybe there is a space in the "Y" block that is hard to bleed. I never thought to look into it when I had it off. Who would any why.||||reg||1
Z||000006||SteveJ||06-18-2001||12:09 PM||||Having done that much bleeding it's hard to beleive that is the problem.||||reg||1
Z||000007||SteveJ||06-18-2001||12:15 PM||||Ya know the first part of your post you mention soft brakes. Isn't that a classic air in the lines symtom? . . .and later you mention the petal going to the floor. Isn't that another?<BR>But it should pump up and that's confusing it doesn't. We would say it's a bad MC but you replaced it.||||reg||1
Z||000008||SteveJ||06-18-2001||12:17 PM||||Well if you replace your booster and it fixes things let me know and I'll do mine.||||reg||1
Z||000009||SteveJ||06-18-2001||12:22 PM||||&gt;&gt;But if I press it with both feet - Im talking really hard here, it will go to the floor. <P>You suppose you get a hard petal fron the front brakes and by pushing harder you get past the proportioning valve to the rears where it sinks due to air?||||reg||1
