Z||000011||SteveJ||06-18-2001||12:26 PM||||.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Ken Wittlief||06-18-2001||01:34 PM||||hey now thats a possibility that makes sense, to a degree anyway. Its only when you stomp down hard on em, or the vacuum assist pushes them down hard, that the proportiong valve opens the front to the rear. (Im not saying this is how its suppose to work - but this would explain whats happening)<P>  or maybe that valve is screwed up and letting the fluid thats suppose to stay in the front leak into the back lines?<P>  Maybe PO put it in backwards?!  - no that cant be, i think the front brakes T off the valve and the rear takes a single line from there.<P>   maybe its simply shot.<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 06-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||theogre||06-18-2001||07:37 PM||||The amount of MC/booster flex doen't sound right. I'd start looking for loose hardware then look for a cracket bracket or something.<P>Mushy pedal, Read my brake service article... The MC can be a B__ch to bleed. the article explains why. <P>You could also have air in the short lines from the MC to the ballance. that air is often hard to get out unless you pressure or vacuum bleed the system.||||reg||1
Z||000014||SteveJ||06-18-2001||07:55 PM||||That flex may not seem right to you but I have seen it and seen it resolved. I wasn't looking for it though and could have lived without it.||||reg||1
Z||000015||SteveJ||06-18-2001||08:34 PM||||Ya know when I first saw that MC rock back and fourth I was aghast. But it resolved itself with bleeding so I figured I had just learned something. <BR>Upon reflection I see your point. That degree of movement seems incorrect even if it does resolve somehow. <P>I have never made any bones about my dissatisfaction with the Fiero brakes. Ken too is dissatisfied. Perhaps there is something wrong with Ken's and mine that shows these symptoms. I did look it over and saw no obvious weakness that would account for this.||||reg||1
